{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791561467,"nonce":"cTTn0Kb9Y9kCLD+bDg1GqNJGq4Q=","message":"Section 1, idle part: 184f5b4210f10fe858e5cc691b4d96f12bbcbef7.\n\n- VM::blocking_wait is the declared blocking wait; TimeCore.sleep and the scheduler's idle wait (Sched::idle) use it. An IdleClock keeps the idle span across waits while running is at most 1/20 of it.\n- 300 ms into an idle span, the wait collects if \u003e= 8 MB were allocated since the last collection. Whenever any collection ran since the last trim, it then hands the freed Immix blocks back (new runtime-seam slot trim; RT_VERSION 6 -\u003e 7) and trims malloc: malloc_zone_pressure_relief on macOS, malloc_trim on glibc.\n- It stays off while collect_suppressed() (a native holds values a collection would not see) or while other threads run. WLIFT_GC_IDLE=0 turns it off.\n\nMeasured on this Mac with vmmap (dirty resident; phys_footprint here includes swap and varies between runs). 2M short-lived strings, then a sleep, interpreter: TOTAL dirty 25.9 MB -\u003e 977 KB; GC heap VM_ALLOCATE dirty 9.3 MB -\u003e 32 KB; DefaultMallocZone dirty 11.4 MB -\u003e 240 KB, allocated 10.1 -\u003e 5.2 MB. In that run the last allocation-driven collection ran within ~1 MB of the burst's end, so the idle wait only handed back. The gain is the burst's freed blocks and malloc'd string buffers, which non-quiet collections had kept.\n\nLeft in section 1: idle waits for channel receive, isolate join, thread waits, and a C API for a host's event loop; charging foreign objects' native memory to the trigger. Sections 2 and 3 untouched.","files":null}]}